WEBVTT

00:01:45.919 --> 00:01:47.119
How's it going, mom?

00:01:49.029 --> 00:01:50.229
Oh, you're muted.

00:01:56.579 --> 00:01:57.299
Turn on my light.

00:01:57.379 --> 00:01:58.099
It's here somewhere.

00:01:58.259 --> 00:01:58.659
Hello.

00:01:58.739 --> 00:01:58.899
Hello.

00:01:58.979 --> 00:01:59.459
Hello.

00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:00.979
Hello.

00:02:02.259 --> 00:02:03.379
You're still muted, man.

00:02:03.699 --> 00:02:05.059
Yeah, Zoom is being done.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:08.819
I found a way to unmute myself, but it wouldn't let me.

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:16.099
It wouldn't let me bring my, like I was trying to make sure I was on the latest version and find where settings are now, and I can't find it.

00:02:16.419 --> 00:02:27.379
But in addition to that, it was once I brought that other window up, it like yeated this whole thing up into a little bitty window in the upper right-hand corner of my screen that I couldn't see.

00:02:28.339 --> 00:02:30.659
Anyway, I don't use Zoom every day anymore.

00:02:31.059 --> 00:02:33.219
Like most of our company runs on Hangouts.

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:36.819
And so like I get confused.

00:02:38.419 --> 00:02:40.979
I didn't realize Hangouts was still around.

00:02:41.299 --> 00:02:42.579
Or Google Meet or whatever.

00:02:42.659 --> 00:02:42.979
I don't know.

00:02:43.059 --> 00:02:44.099
It's the same damn thing.

00:02:44.419 --> 00:02:44.659
Yeah.

00:02:44.979 --> 00:02:45.779
Google Talk.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:47.299
That's what I worked on.

00:02:47.539 --> 00:02:48.179
That's what we called it.

00:02:48.259 --> 00:02:49.139
I don't know.

00:02:50.259 --> 00:02:58.659
There's one person I talked to over Google, whatever it's called, and that's my sister in Japan.

00:02:59.619 --> 00:03:02.339
I don't know why, but it seems to be what we settled on.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.819
I use it for a science fiction book club month thing.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.620
We all get on Google Meet for that.

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:16.339
And then at work, I use WebEx because I work for Cisco.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:17.620
I'm sorry.

00:03:17.859 --> 00:03:20.019
Well, WebEx is like literally the worst.

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:21.859
It's gotten a lot better.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:22.899
It's still not great.

00:03:22.979 --> 00:03:24.500
It's better than Teams.

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:25.379
Oh, Teams.

00:03:26.259 --> 00:03:31.859
I log into Teams and I'm like, there's always something fucked up with like Microsoft accounts and stuff, and I can never figure it out.

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:36.419
So I always give up and I'm just like, just do the web thing and don't bother logging in.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:37.219
Right.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:51.859
See, I always think of this radio program committee meeting where somebody had written this paper that was all about, you know, here's this great video conferencing product we could make.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:04:00.979
And they and the person talking about the paper listed off all the lists of features that they wanted.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:07.539
And then somebody else in the program committee stood up and said, you know, WebEx actually has all those features.

00:04:07.699 --> 00:04:09.459
We just don't pay for them.

00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:14.659
And we shut down the paper like that.

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:16.740
I really wish you could.

00:04:18.179 --> 00:04:22.740
I will say one thing about WebEx that it does probably better than Zoom.

00:04:23.059 --> 00:04:27.459
It works very well if you have a very shitty internet connection.

00:04:29.549 --> 00:04:34.349
It will just drop the frame rate to like three frames a second and keep the audio going.

00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:34.989
Oh, yeah.

00:04:35.549 --> 00:04:35.709
It won't.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.309
It's not going to prioritize audio.

00:04:37.469 --> 00:04:37.629
Yeah.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:38.029
Yeah.

00:04:40.299 --> 00:04:40.620
Yeah.

00:04:44.379 --> 00:04:45.259
How y'all doing?

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:46.699
Anything new going on?

00:04:47.659 --> 00:04:49.659
My whole company is for York.

00:04:50.379 --> 00:04:52.219
So I used to work.

00:04:53.419 --> 00:04:59.099
Do you guys remember Tom Gillis, the GM for NSX and security?

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:00.299
Yeah.

00:05:00.939 --> 00:05:02.699
Yeah, yeah, I do.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:09.099
I worked for Craig Connors, who was like the guy that came in with Bella Cloud.

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:10.779
I worked for him under Tom.

00:05:11.099 --> 00:05:21.740
And Tom just got switched out as the GM for that group because they did a big reorg under all of the product stuff and is going to be Cisco's new CTO.

00:05:23.579 --> 00:05:27.179
I wonder, like, what does the job of a CTO look like at a company?

00:05:27.339 --> 00:05:39.740
Well, I think for, I mean, Tom's technical in that, like, early in his career, he wrote guidance software for the Patriot missiles, but like hasn't really done, like, then he went into the business side of things and stayed in the business side the whole time.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:49.979
So I think he's in more of the chief talking officer side, like where he's, you know, you have CTOs talk to CTOs and like they all pretend that they used to be engineers.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:50.939
Yeah.

00:05:51.899 --> 00:05:52.059
Yeah.

00:05:52.219 --> 00:06:01.099
So there's some of that going on, but my favorite was every time that God, I'm blanking on his name.

00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:04.219
Like old CEO of VMware.

00:06:05.179 --> 00:06:05.339
God.

00:06:05.659 --> 00:06:05.979
Greg?

00:06:06.219 --> 00:06:06.699
With that?

00:06:07.179 --> 00:06:07.819
Greg?

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:09.179
No, no, no, no.

00:06:09.419 --> 00:06:10.059
What's that?

00:06:10.379 --> 00:06:11.899
No, the one that bought our company.

00:06:11.979 --> 00:06:13.259
I blank it on his name.

00:06:13.579 --> 00:06:14.379
Hawktan?

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:16.379
No, the one before that.

00:06:17.019 --> 00:06:18.699
Our company as in Heptio.

00:06:20.059 --> 00:06:20.860
Gelsinger?

00:06:21.099 --> 00:06:21.659
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:21.899 --> 00:06:25.259
So every time Pat would talk, he would love to talk about how he's still a real engineer.

00:06:25.339 --> 00:06:29.259
And we'd all nod along, like, yeah, dude, you're still like, you know, you're super technical still.

00:06:29.419 --> 00:06:29.819
Don't worry.

00:06:30.459 --> 00:06:32.059
And he would start like, he'd be talking software.

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:35.019
He's like, yeah, I really, you know, I would talk speeds and feeds.

00:06:35.099 --> 00:06:37.099
I'm like, that's not a thing in software.

00:06:37.259 --> 00:06:39.500
We don't, we don't do speeds and feeds and software.

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:48.860
You know, at my startup at Nasira, those who worked on speeds and feeds and software raised a hand in objection.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:52.539
Just for the record.

00:06:53.019 --> 00:06:54.699
Like CNC control software.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:55.419
I mean, like.

00:06:57.019 --> 00:07:01.019
Some of us did worry about speeds and feeds, but we will digress.

00:07:02.219 --> 00:07:13.899
I wrote the very first ever data plane for a network service device, basically a firewall that was embedded in a modular switch.

00:07:14.219 --> 00:07:16.620
And that you very much had to worry about speeds and feeds.

00:07:16.779 --> 00:07:19.740
Well, what do speeds and feeds mean in that context?

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:26.539
Basically, I have a back plane that can throw 256 gigabits at me, and I'm trying to keep up.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:29.179
And all I can actually do is about a gigabit.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.740
No, speeds and feeds, you're talking about CNC, where you actually have things spinning, which is the speed.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:34.379
Oh, right, right.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.100
And then the feed is like how fast do you move it across the part?

00:07:37.659 --> 00:07:51.659
Yeah, people stole it in the network world and speeds and feeds to mean like bandwidth and like, yeah, so basically they move from machine shops into networking parlance.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:08:01.899
Yes, that's literally where I stumbled across it as well, where I worked on a network packet thing that shipped did a gigabit network speeds on a Celeron.

00:08:02.219 --> 00:08:02.459
Okay.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:07.100
Yeah, and all the networking people got pissed off that we took over the term control plane.

00:08:08.939 --> 00:08:10.779
No, we give them credit.

00:08:16.139 --> 00:08:22.699
Costanas, can you accept my friend request on Discord so I can add some DMs with who are you?

00:08:24.139 --> 00:08:26.139
My woggle bug.

00:08:26.299 --> 00:08:28.379
That's my Discord name.

00:08:29.259 --> 00:08:30.699
Why do I not have you?

00:08:31.019 --> 00:08:32.059
I don't know.

00:08:32.379 --> 00:08:35.179
I don't see why is one password not letting me log in.

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:39.980
Discord basically hides the friend requests, so you have to dig a bit to even find them.

00:08:41.339 --> 00:08:41.819
Pending.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:45.259
We weren't fun.

00:08:45.579 --> 00:08:46.859
We weren't friends until today.

00:08:47.099 --> 00:08:47.740
How sad?

00:08:47.979 --> 00:08:53.979
Well, it's almost meaningless on Discord, except for DMing people who you aren't in a common server with, something like that.

00:08:54.299 --> 00:08:54.859
Yes.

00:08:55.819 --> 00:08:57.899
It's always very fascinating.

00:08:58.539 --> 00:09:03.979
So my computer was off long enough that one password needed to be restarted twice to update multiple times.

00:09:04.299 --> 00:09:05.740
That would make it two password.

00:09:06.059 --> 00:09:06.779
I know, right?

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:09.979
Just for the record.

00:09:10.779 --> 00:09:14.939
Just for the record, Mike, you never worked at a startup, but I don't think I quite understood that.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.620
So I worked at a very small company.

00:09:18.939 --> 00:09:20.539
So I did a startup-y thing.

00:09:20.859 --> 00:09:27.579
When I was at UT and I was getting my electrical engineering degree, there was this entrepreneur guy that has like a serial entrepreneur.

00:09:27.659 --> 00:09:29.259
He starts a bunch of different companies.

00:09:30.539 --> 00:09:33.259
I'm going to try to condense this story as small as possible.

00:09:33.579 --> 00:09:39.740
He was at IBM selling them process equipment for a company he reped.

00:09:40.059 --> 00:09:47.259
And they had this laser that had all these, this laser chassis thing, and it had all these stickers on it saying, do not use decommissioning.

00:09:47.659 --> 00:09:48.539
And he asked what happened.

00:09:48.859 --> 00:09:52.459
Somebody didn't follow the rules and they overrode the thing and it caused a safety incident.

00:09:52.539 --> 00:09:59.740
And it was like a Nidium GAG 100 watt laser with like a four axis robot.

00:10:00.459 --> 00:10:01.740
Now we're talking speeds and feeds.

00:10:02.059 --> 00:10:03.500
Now we're talking speeds and feeds.

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:05.019
And he bought it from them.

00:10:05.179 --> 00:10:06.379
He's like, well, what are you guys going to do with it?

00:10:06.459 --> 00:10:08.219
And they're like, we're just going to decommission and get out of here.

00:10:08.299 --> 00:10:09.899
And he bought it from them for cheap.

00:10:10.139 --> 00:10:11.899
And then he paid to get it moved.

00:10:12.219 --> 00:10:17.019
And then he had this bright idea because he was all involved in surface mount stuff of like when you build.

00:10:17.259 --> 00:10:19.259
So 100 watt lasers are very bright.

00:10:19.579 --> 00:10:20.620
Yes, very bright.

00:10:20.859 --> 00:10:32.699
And so when you build stencils for like squeegeeing solder paste onto a surface mount board, you have to have these little voids and you squeegee over the voids.

00:10:32.779 --> 00:10:42.059
And the way that that's typically done is they do this like photo thing and then drop acid on it and it eats in from both sides and you end up with something that looks like an hourglass.

00:10:42.379 --> 00:10:50.779
And then you squeegee through the hourglass in the middle of the hourglass causes like volume problems because sometimes it takes the top half back up with it.

00:10:51.019 --> 00:10:54.539
And he's like, hey, if we shoot it from a laser and we make it more like a trapezoid.

00:10:54.619 --> 00:11:14.220
So anyway, cutting this to a much shorter conversation, while I was in school, I programmed the thing, bought a bunch of like various different kinds of nickel encoded steel for the what they use for the stencils because they have to be squeegeed a bunch and started firing at steel with this laser and trying to figure things out with microscopes.

00:11:14.459 --> 00:11:16.220
You know that steel is reflective.

00:11:16.859 --> 00:11:17.179
Yeah, yeah.

00:11:17.339 --> 00:11:20.299
And I had I had the goggles on, like back to the future.

00:11:20.539 --> 00:11:30.939
Anyway, did all that stuff and then quickly realized that Nidium YAG is not the right type of laser to drill steel and told him that I like, unfortunately, I filled this out.

00:11:31.179 --> 00:11:32.059
He's like, oh, that's okay.

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:33.659
I kind of thought this might go sideways.

00:11:33.819 --> 00:11:38.939
So I already talked to Texas State Technical College and I had an independent appraiser come in and appraise the laser.

00:11:39.179 --> 00:11:44.220
And with the tax write-off I'm going to get from donating it to the college, I'm going to end up making like $40,000.

00:11:44.539 --> 00:11:48.459
And that was plenty to pay you part-time to figure out if we could actually turn this into a business.

00:11:48.779 --> 00:11:50.139
And so he donated it off.

00:11:50.379 --> 00:11:56.939
And then he's like, oh, by the way, I've decided I want to sell this other business because it's taking a lot of my time and I want to go fishing.

00:11:58.459 --> 00:12:01.980
Will you come computerize the business and run it for me?

00:12:02.299 --> 00:12:08.299
And then I'll tell them that you're the general manager and they don't need me and I'll sell the business and you can run it.

00:12:08.619 --> 00:12:10.539
And I said, okay.

00:12:10.859 --> 00:12:11.579
And so I did that.

00:12:11.819 --> 00:12:12.299
Wait a second.

00:12:12.379 --> 00:12:12.699
Wait a second.

00:12:12.779 --> 00:12:13.259
Wait a second.

00:12:13.339 --> 00:12:18.539
You did not work at a startup and yet had several quintessential Silicon Valley startup stories.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:27.480
I mean, all I'm waiting for here and now is to find out that there was a safe, you know.

00:12:30.279 --> 00:12:34.120
So, anyway, took that job, ran the computerized the company.

00:12:34.279 --> 00:12:40.759
This is Windows for Work Groups 311 and NetTastic over 9,600 baud modems.

00:12:40.919 --> 00:12:42.120
I'm just dating myself.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:46.200
Computerized it, got all this going, ran the business.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:49.159
He did, true to his word, sold the business.

00:12:49.399 --> 00:12:50.440
The new guys came in.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:58.600
This is back when investment banking firms would like buy up a bunch of smom and pops that were the same and then try to package them and IPO them and profit.

00:12:59.399 --> 00:13:00.279
Private equity, okay.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:00.519
Yeah.

00:13:00.759 --> 00:13:00.840
Yeah.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:02.759
This is before they were private equity.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.919
Anyway, so I did all that.

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:06.919
They loved the way I was running mine.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:13.799
So then I ended up running all of operations like west of the Mississippi and hated it because I wanted to do nerd stuff.

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:17.799
And like I had all these factory workers and like I was like, no.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:27.159
So I took like a 70% pay cut and went to become a contractor for the Air Force Information Warfare Center because captain sounded cool and I wanted to go do that.

00:13:27.399 --> 00:13:30.440
Moved to San Antonio and restarted my career.

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:42.200
Did that met a bunch of people, some of which were the people that founded Wheel Group, which was an IDS that got acquired by Cisco.

00:13:42.519 --> 00:13:47.000
So then when I wanted to move back to Austin, I called some of them and they're like, yeah, you can come work for Cisco.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:49.879
And then I went to Cisco, Juniper, VMware, back to Cisco.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:51.000
Oh, that's funny.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.480
Did you know Justin Pettit?

00:13:53.559 --> 00:13:54.600
I think he might have worked for Wheel Good.

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:56.200
Yeah, yeah, you know, Justin.

00:13:56.279 --> 00:13:56.440
Okay.

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:57.000
Yeah.

00:13:57.639 --> 00:13:58.039
He was.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:00.919
But this is what I tell people when they're like early in their career.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.200
I'm like, if all you do is chase money, you're going to end up in a place that you hate, right?

00:14:04.279 --> 00:14:06.039
At some point, you got to figure out what do you want to do.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:11.080
Because like that job that you landed in sounds like one of those jobs where I'm sure it could pay well.

00:14:11.399 --> 00:14:11.559
Yeah.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.000
And it's pure hell.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:13.559
Yeah.

00:14:13.879 --> 00:14:17.799
It paid amazingly well for a what was I at the time?

00:14:18.039 --> 00:14:19.240
22 year old or something?

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:20.759
Because I went to school early.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:22.919
Like I graduated.

00:14:23.559 --> 00:14:25.720
I was, yeah, so I would have been 22.

00:14:25.879 --> 00:14:27.879
Yeah, I was 22 and I had that job.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:34.600
And I just remembered I have to send a note to Mark Johnson to tell him that he's not the only high school dropout I knew at VMware.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:39.879
This is a running game.

00:14:40.279 --> 00:14:41.879
He doesn't want everybody to know, Cassado.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:42.759
I won't tell him.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:44.120
I won't tell him.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:46.279
Hold on.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:48.039
I will tell him that he was not.

00:14:48.279 --> 00:14:49.240
Actually, I'll even make it worse.

00:14:49.319 --> 00:14:52.680
He was not the only distinguished engineer who was a high school dropout.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:54.759
True.

00:14:56.039 --> 00:15:01.159
I find that a lot of people who are very bright get very bored with high school very easily.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:04.759
I may have actually dropped out of high school if I hadn't found this.

00:15:05.639 --> 00:15:11.319
Texas had this program where you could do your junior and your senior year of high school at university.

00:15:11.639 --> 00:15:15.240
And so my high school diploma is from the University of North Texas.

00:15:15.559 --> 00:15:16.759
Oh, that's cool.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:17.559
All right.

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:29.319
As much as I would enjoy having this conversation until the wee orbors of the morning, I think, and I, and as much as I feel my son would then say that we're like that episode of The Simpsons.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:31.879
So I have a question, though, before you get going.

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:34.680
Have I worked at a startup?

00:15:37.159 --> 00:15:39.559
Not a Silicon Valley venture-funded startup.

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:46.600
This is why I said no, but I kind of have an extra-I haven't worked at a startup either.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:47.399
Yeah, you did.

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:48.519
You worked at Hiptio.

00:15:48.759 --> 00:15:51.080
Yeah, we were not a Silicon Valley company.

00:15:52.039 --> 00:15:53.480
It was a, it's a, you know, but it.

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:55.060
Read the thing I wrote, Silicon Valley.

00:15:55.379 --> 00:15:56.500
Silken Valley's a state of mind.

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:58.500
Silken Valley's a moon.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.140
A copy of a state of mind.

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:03.300
A geography.

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:09.540
All right.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:11.620
Sorry for the segue.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.580
No, dude, that was actually worth the price of admission.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:16.500
Alay.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.660
Okay, so just to remind you what happened, Alay.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:20.820
All right.

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:25.860
Alay told the surviving thug to ignore the cleric and go after Daz, shouting that the wizard had the key.

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:29.300
Though in truth, Grygum quietly held both the key and the gem.

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:39.540
Daz conjured a massive explosion of fire that engulfed the surviving thug, and the successful detonation sent shockwaves of a different kind through all of Candlekeep.

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:50.580
Bells rang, screams echoed, and scholars scrambled for their notebooks, because the fireball's very existent proved that the library's legendary magical wards had been broken.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.220
Alay, and, you know, papers had to be written.

00:16:53.379 --> 00:16:57.460
Alay killed the wounded thug himself, and then, hands trembling, proposed a surrender.

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:06.820
He revealed he knew the location of a sacred room beneath the keep, the two lines of a cryptogram that mattered, and the name of a finger in Menzoberranzan paying to keep a certain drow breathing.

00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:15.700
He begged not for freedom, but for a cell inside Candlekeep's magic nullifying prison, the one place he believed he might survive what was coming.

00:17:16.019 --> 00:17:22.259
Grygum used restorative magic to bring Zalthir back to conscience, and the party began to consider the wizard's offer.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.140
Before they could act, his entire assassin appeared.

00:17:25.299 --> 00:17:28.900
Alay's hands bowed, looked at the newcomer, and said coolly, Took your time.

00:17:29.059 --> 00:17:29.779
I've been in the room.

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:31.620
I know exactly who is carrying what.

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:33.220
He nodded towards Daz.

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:39.860
The assassin launched a brutal assault, striking Daz again and again until the wizard crumpled unconscious to the floor.

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:48.180
The assassin then searched Daz's body for the sapphire and found nothing, his horror plain on his face, for the gem had long since passed to Grygum.

00:17:48.259 --> 00:17:50.100
Thorin's fury was immediate and absolute.

00:17:50.420 --> 00:18:03.860
He struck the assassin with a menacing blow that filled the man with dread, then unleashed a relentless fury of attacks, flurry of attacks, including a devastating radiant strike that left the assassin crumpled and broken on the high tower floor.

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:15.860
Grygum rushed to Daz and poured healing energy into him, pulling him back from the edge of death, while Alay Evanmore looked on in tearful panic, his last hope of rescue lying unconscious at his feet.

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:21.700
The party secured Alay and stripped his high tower key from him, taking care to not bring the two keys together.

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:24.579
Well, Thorin quietly slipped the key to Zalthir.

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:28.500
What happens if you bring the two keys together?

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:30.340
No, no, that was a typo.

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:35.940
Grygum quietly slipped the key to Zalthir and it was ultimately tucked away inside Glabbagool for safekeeping.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:40.579
The gelatinous coom standing just a little bit taller with pride at the responsibility.

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:44.900
It's a little weird to store things inside our NPCs, but I'll allow it.

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:54.900
Yeah, sensing that remaining in the room would be catastrophic, the party moved Alay out of the high tower lobby, a decision that proved wise moments later when Manshoon himself arrived.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:19:04.740
The legendary wizard conjured a wall of force and shattered the magical door that the two keys were meant to protect, breaking Candlekeep's inner sanctum through sheer overwhelming power.

00:19:05.059 --> 00:19:10.100
He had arrived alone and visibly weakened, having expended enormous magical resources to get here.

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:15.620
But he had arrived, and Alay, trembling in the party's custody, revealed the one card he still held.

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:19.380
He had never told Manshoon the truth about what lay inside the depository.

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:24.340
That secret, and whatever Alay intended to trade for, would have to wait.

00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:25.620
For now.

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:27.779
All right.

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:38.620
Okay, so there's this insanely powerful wizard hiding behind a wall of force, broke has broken into the secret room.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:39.820
And

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:50.990
all right, so the way we're going to do this is we're actually going to use a spreadsheet.

00:19:53.619 --> 00:19:55.379
You're not DD unless you have spreadsheets.

00:19:55.619 --> 00:19:58.819
By the way, I'm looking at, like, who's the babushka woman there?

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.659
Is that on the map?

00:20:04.179 --> 00:20:04.339
Oh.

00:20:07.619 --> 00:20:09.139
Do I have the wrong map?

00:20:09.779 --> 00:20:10.259
That's great.

00:20:10.579 --> 00:20:12.019
Babushka woman.

00:20:12.339 --> 00:20:12.579
Yeah.

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:13.699
I'd like to come over.

00:20:14.339 --> 00:20:16.099
So the assassin Thuggardan.

00:20:17.779 --> 00:20:18.259
Thorn.

00:20:18.899 --> 00:20:19.619
Grygum.

00:20:19.859 --> 00:20:20.339
I can't.

00:20:20.419 --> 00:20:22.819
I don't see anything for Glabbagool or for Daz.

00:20:23.139 --> 00:20:24.980
I mean, this is the map we were using.

00:20:25.299 --> 00:20:27.219
No, I'm just saying when I hover, it's just like.

00:20:27.859 --> 00:20:29.539
Yeah, I don't know who that is either.

00:20:29.779 --> 00:20:30.819
Let me just see.

00:20:31.459 --> 00:20:32.500
It's a great icon.

00:20:33.939 --> 00:20:34.500
Yes.

00:20:45.319 --> 00:20:45.559
No.

00:20:46.199 --> 00:20:47.159
I just, I don't know.

00:20:49.399 --> 00:20:50.759
Oh, she's gone.

00:20:51.079 --> 00:20:51.959
Oh, okay.

00:20:52.119 --> 00:20:53.639
Yeah, I remember this one now, too.

00:20:53.959 --> 00:20:54.279
All right.

00:20:54.519 --> 00:20:54.679
Okay.

00:20:54.919 --> 00:20:56.279
The babushka.

00:20:58.119 --> 00:20:58.919
No, that was something else.

00:20:59.079 --> 00:20:59.799
It's another map.

00:21:00.119 --> 00:21:00.759
All right.

00:21:01.719 --> 00:21:03.959
So Alay Evanmore is here.

00:21:04.359 --> 00:21:12.519
They're all just so elaborate and carefully designed that, you know, it's sometimes I can't tell which one we should be on.

00:21:13.719 --> 00:21:16.119
Are you making fun of my scrolls?

00:21:16.839 --> 00:21:20.809
Hey, mine would be just as bad.

00:21:21.129 --> 00:21:21.529
All right.

00:21:22.169 --> 00:21:22.409
Okay.

00:21:22.569 --> 00:21:22.969
There we go.

00:21:23.129 --> 00:21:23.529
There we are.

00:21:23.929 --> 00:21:25.209
This is the high tower.

00:21:25.689 --> 00:21:27.289
The wall of force is.

00:21:27.449 --> 00:21:28.649
Let me just do this.

00:21:29.929 --> 00:21:31.369
Extra large.

00:21:33.619 --> 00:21:39.139
There was a wall of force, and it is sufficiently large such that all of Gabe's mathematics will be proven to be insufficient.

00:21:39.459 --> 00:21:47.219
And you should not spend time trying to carefully point out how I got the line wrong.

00:21:47.539 --> 00:21:49.139
That sounds like a challenge.

00:21:49.459 --> 00:21:50.259
No, no, no.

00:21:50.339 --> 00:21:58.179
I'm simply stating that anything I don't this person over here is Tadric.

00:21:58.339 --> 00:21:59.059
Sorry.

00:22:02.129 --> 00:22:03.489
That's who that is.

00:22:05.009 --> 00:22:07.169
That's who the Babushka woman is.

00:22:12.239 --> 00:22:13.199
There we go.

00:22:13.839 --> 00:22:14.159
Okay.

00:22:14.719 --> 00:22:16.799
So Manshoon shows up.

00:22:17.119 --> 00:22:30.749
And just to make this a little bit interesting, the way this will work, just to create a sense of urgency, because I'm told that's a great management technique.

00:22:34.459 --> 00:22:35.019
All right.

00:22:39.549 --> 00:22:39.869
All right.

00:22:40.109 --> 00:22:44.109
So imagine something that's going to happen.

00:22:44.429 --> 00:22:46.429
Depending on the actions you take,

00:22:54.199 --> 00:23:07.799
you will either intersect with Manshoon or he will be able to stop him or he will get what he's he will get what he came for.

00:23:08.119 --> 00:23:09.399
So here.

00:23:11.639 --> 00:23:13.399
Imagine there is six boxes.

00:23:13.559 --> 00:23:16.279
I am not going to draw this because, wait, there's a spreadsheet.

00:23:16.359 --> 00:23:17.719
We do have a spreadsheet.

00:23:19.319 --> 00:23:21.799
I just realized we do, in fact, have a spreadsheet.

00:23:22.119 --> 00:23:27.239
But for the purposes of this exercise, let's just imagine Manshoon is there are six boxes.

00:23:29.079 --> 00:23:31.879
Manshoon is on box one.

00:23:38.669 --> 00:23:41.709
You, party, on box zero.

00:23:44.099 --> 00:23:47.219
Manshoon gets to box six.

00:23:48.499 --> 00:23:49.699
He wins.

00:23:51.909 --> 00:23:52.309
All right.

00:23:53.909 --> 00:23:55.589
Just imagine what?

00:23:55.909 --> 00:23:57.509
How should I imagine these boxes?

00:23:57.589 --> 00:23:59.029
Are they just suggesting?

00:23:59.189 --> 00:24:01.589
No, no, there are things that can happen.

00:24:01.749 --> 00:24:05.989
So your goal is to figure out what he's up to and stop him before he gets it.

00:24:07.189 --> 00:24:07.509
Okay.

00:24:08.149 --> 00:24:08.309
Right.

00:24:08.469 --> 00:24:13.759
And there are multiple ways to stop him, including killing him.

00:24:14.079 --> 00:24:15.199
Always a popular way.

00:24:15.519 --> 00:24:20.399
And depending on which paths you take, you may or may not meet him before the end.

00:24:21.599 --> 00:24:21.919
Okay.

00:24:22.559 --> 00:24:25.679
And because this is this party, I have no contingency plan.

00:24:25.759 --> 00:24:38.959
So if you do something completely off the rails, it may involve me going back to my drawing board and going, hmm, and doing re and pulling off one of my DD books, you know, which will make me very happy, but it might cause a pause in the game.

00:24:41.359 --> 00:24:42.719
I would be delighted if you did that.

00:24:42.879 --> 00:24:46.239
So just to be clear, this would be awesome if you guys did something completely unexpected.

00:24:46.559 --> 00:24:51.279
But I just want you to be aware that it might take me a moment to jump back onto the scene.

00:24:51.599 --> 00:24:52.239
All right.

00:24:53.199 --> 00:24:53.519
Okay.

00:24:53.679 --> 00:24:54.959
So you're now sitting with Alay.

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:02.060
All right.

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:05.019
Okay.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:08.060
So Alay goes, he's not looking for me.

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:09.900
That's the part you don't understand.

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:14.620
He sent a courier 11 years and the courier wear a glove.

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:15.820
And I never saw the face.

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:18.460
And I told myself that was discretion.

00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:21.630
It was not discretion.

00:25:21.789 --> 00:25:24.029
It was that I wasn't worth the face.

00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:28.110
Basically, he's discovered that he's been a patsy this whole time.

00:25:28.269 --> 00:25:29.630
He thought he was an important person.

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:32.750
He literally thought that Manshoon was going to show up to kill him.

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:34.350
He thought the assassin was coming for that.

00:25:34.509 --> 00:25:36.350
Now he's realized he's been a patsy.

00:25:36.509 --> 00:25:38.430
The whole time he's been a patsy.

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:40.190
It's not even worth killing.

00:25:40.509 --> 00:25:41.710
No, it's not even worth killing.

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:46.190
Like, Manshoon shows up and doesn't even waste the time to kill him.

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:48.670
Not worth the price of the bullet.

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:50.190
Right, exactly.

00:25:50.509 --> 00:25:52.430
So this is, you know, his ego, his.

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:54.269
Not worth the spell slot.

00:25:54.590 --> 00:25:54.829
He wasn't.

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:56.269
Yes, not worth the spell slot.

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:58.350
He wasn't worth the straight.

00:26:02.039 --> 00:26:02.359
Okay.

00:26:02.919 --> 00:26:03.240
So

00:26:07.729 --> 00:26:08.689
what is it?

00:26:09.329 --> 00:26:12.769
So he's like, here's what I want.

00:26:14.289 --> 00:26:14.849
I want.

00:26:15.169 --> 00:26:15.729
I want.

00:26:15.969 --> 00:26:16.449
I want.

00:26:16.769 --> 00:26:20.129
I want to be put in the prison of the...

00:26:21.009 --> 00:26:25.889
I want you to take me and put me in the null magic prism of Candlekeep.

00:26:26.049 --> 00:26:27.969
It's the only thing that'll keep me alive.

00:26:28.609 --> 00:26:37.089
Well, before we can do that, we're going to need your help with Manshoon and how we can find him and stop him.

00:26:37.409 --> 00:26:37.649
Right.

00:26:37.809 --> 00:26:41.489
Just to remind you, what was his offer last week?

00:26:41.569 --> 00:26:42.769
I just got to remember myself a little bit.

00:26:43.169 --> 00:26:45.169
Because without some help, I have no fucking clue.

00:26:45.489 --> 00:26:45.969
Right.

00:26:47.649 --> 00:26:50.609
So what Alay offered.

00:26:51.729 --> 00:26:52.289
Just check that.

00:26:52.369 --> 00:26:53.009
Give me a second.

00:26:53.249 --> 00:26:54.849
I have this written down.

00:26:56.289 --> 00:26:57.249
It's a little funny pattern.

00:27:01.459 --> 00:27:24.499
So what Manshoon, what he offered, he's offering a bunch of things, but things that he offered you guys that you may have forgotten, because, of course, you do not have the privilege of sitting in front of all of these notes that I have, is that he claims to know who Daz is related to.

00:27:26.339 --> 00:27:30.499
You have the suspicion that he knows about who Daz is really after.

00:27:35.059 --> 00:27:36.419
So who's really after Daz?

00:27:36.739 --> 00:27:37.459
Yeah, who's really after?

00:27:37.779 --> 00:27:40.099
Who is the person who's really after me?

00:27:40.419 --> 00:27:42.579
Yeah, like, why is Daz still alive?

00:27:43.779 --> 00:27:44.019
Right?

00:27:44.499 --> 00:27:49.699
Like, who paid for these very expensive people to keep you alive?

00:27:50.339 --> 00:27:50.659
Right?

00:27:50.979 --> 00:27:52.579
That's one thing he might know.

00:27:53.059 --> 00:28:00.899
The second thing he might know, the second thing he says he knows is the real reason why Manshoon showed up here.

00:28:11.439 --> 00:28:16.319
And so he knows those two things.

00:28:19.329 --> 00:28:19.969
All right.

00:28:21.009 --> 00:28:22.529
He's willing to trade.

00:28:23.809 --> 00:28:25.249
Well, the one.

00:28:26.049 --> 00:28:28.769
And you know, just to be clear, Tadrick's there.

00:28:28.849 --> 00:28:33.409
So if you want to hand him over to Tadrick, Tadrick can take him into custody.

00:28:33.729 --> 00:28:37.969
If you tie his hands up and shove a sock in his mouth, he can't cast any spells anyway.

00:28:39.409 --> 00:28:50.129
Well, the one thing might be important to Daz, but I think knowing what Manshoon is up to is more important for the goal of the adventure here.

00:28:51.489 --> 00:28:56.689
He wants this in exchange for basically not being tortured and killed.

00:28:58.049 --> 00:29:02.289
Very specifically, he wants to be put into the prison.

00:29:02.609 --> 00:29:02.769
Right.

00:29:03.089 --> 00:29:07.009
The most important thing he wants is to be put into the prison, because the most important thing he wants is to...

00:29:10.049 --> 00:29:12.209
We're in the process of rolling the new character, so.

00:29:12.609 --> 00:29:13.649
Don't be so loud.

00:29:13.889 --> 00:29:14.209
Okay.

00:29:17.409 --> 00:29:30.529
So the thing that he wants is, so as you recall at the very beginning of this adventure, when he wanted to put Little Drow in prison, Candlekeep has got null magic prisms.

00:29:33.619 --> 00:29:43.379
So, Alay Ivanmoor thinks the safest place on Toril and in the north is inside of one of those null magic prisms.

00:29:44.019 --> 00:29:48.180
Because nobody else can use the magic there either, and so he'd be safe from it.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:54.740
Yes, and so if you're trying to protect yourself against a powerful wizard, that feels like the safest place in all of creation.

00:29:55.059 --> 00:29:58.259
I won't mention to him that that failed here.

00:29:58.819 --> 00:30:00.899
No, this is not the time you can.

00:30:01.059 --> 00:30:01.859
I'm just saying.

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:03.379
Oh, right.

00:30:03.539 --> 00:30:05.539
It's just the wrong time to mention it.

00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:07.059
All right.

00:30:08.899 --> 00:30:11.859
So, so that's what he wants.

00:30:12.500 --> 00:30:13.379
Tadrick.

00:30:14.419 --> 00:30:15.940
I'm willing to say that.

00:30:16.099 --> 00:30:18.899
It's no skin off me.

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:19.779
All right.

00:30:23.299 --> 00:30:24.659
The rest of you.

00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:27.629
Now, do you want to give that to him?

00:30:27.869 --> 00:30:32.349
He's, by the way, he's, what do you want to give it in exchange for?

00:30:34.509 --> 00:30:38.349
Well, we definitely need to know everything that he knows about Manshoon.

00:30:38.589 --> 00:30:54.509
Daz, do you want to ask for your clue on who is funding both the protection and the trying to keep me alive and the folks that are trying to keep me unalive?

00:30:59.620 --> 00:31:04.500
So you want both of those pieces if he knows them.

00:31:04.580 --> 00:31:05.620
Like, I don't know what he knows.

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:11.620
I think every little bit of information we get right now helps draw a picture of the puzzle.

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:12.580
I see.

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:19.299
I mean, what do we have to offer besides what he's already asked for?

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:20.019
Right.

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:31.779
He looks at you and says, well, what guarantees will I have that I will be placed in the null cell?

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:39.860
Can we just, I don't know, take him there and hand him over.

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:45.220
I mean, like, like, you know, like, he's not in the position to negotiate it.

00:31:46.019 --> 00:31:47.539
No, he's not, but he's an academic.

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:49.620
So there's another officer there.

00:31:49.779 --> 00:31:50.820
You could tell Tadric.

00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:53.380
You can talk to Tadric, encourage Tadric to do it.

00:31:53.539 --> 00:31:56.019
I mean, you don't have to take him there.

00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:56.500
You can.

00:31:57.059 --> 00:31:58.100
All right, Tadric.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:31:59.860
This is how this has got to be.

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:05.460
We need to defeat Manshoon or the entire tower is at risk.

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:14.900
We're in a hurry here and we've got to make compromises that we otherwise might not want to make.

00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:26.340
Tadric is like, Tadric realizes that you have been deputized by Kalan Strongbranch and you have defeated Mazi Codo, saved his life.

00:32:27.140 --> 00:32:29.860
Look, and I founded an entire new philosophy.

00:32:30.579 --> 00:32:30.900
He paused.

00:32:31.059 --> 00:32:31.940
He does account for something.

00:32:33.539 --> 00:32:34.500
Will we write a monograph?

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:36.100
Do a graph together?

00:32:37.460 --> 00:32:38.100
Yes, yes.

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:42.100
We can do a two-person paper.

00:32:43.460 --> 00:32:46.900
It seems like I've got enough prestige here that it'll likely be accepted.

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:54.820
But you never know with the whole anonymous submission thing, but I've got a pretty good track record.

00:32:56.900 --> 00:32:58.820
That was very well delivered.

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:01.300
Tadric.

00:33:04.420 --> 00:33:07.380
I think I could have poked you in the middle of the night and you would have delivered that.

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:10.180
I've been on program committees before.

00:33:11.700 --> 00:33:12.980
That was very well delivered.

00:33:13.460 --> 00:33:14.740
Tadric signs off.

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:16.340
Tadric is relieved.

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:16.980
Very pleased.

00:33:17.140 --> 00:33:17.539
Sorry.

00:33:19.059 --> 00:33:20.900
So Manshoon, sorry.

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:22.740
Kalan has finally relieved.

00:33:22.900 --> 00:33:23.620
He's like, this is.

00:33:23.860 --> 00:33:25.940
And yeah, that is something I could mumble into my pillow.

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:34.180
Yeah, he has that look on his face of somebody who's been who realizes that his whole, he thought he was the kingpin.

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:38.340
He thought he was the man, but now he realized he was just a pawn in other people's plans.

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:52.180
And so he tells you that there's a secret room beneath the keep, which everybody didn't know about, that holds a bunch of artifacts of extraordinary power.

00:33:52.500 --> 00:33:56.980
And that the way to them is by solving a cryptogram.

